 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 520310,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "In a hypothetical research situation, scientists discover bacterial endospores in silt at the bottom of a marsh that has been contaminated with a pollutant for 25 years. The silt of the marsh was deposited in annual layers. This means that the age of the endospores can be estimated by identifying the layer of silt in which the endospores are found. In Flask A, researchers place 20-year-old endospores along with growth medium and the pollutant. In Flask B, researchers place 100-year-old endospores along with growth medium and the pollutant. Suppose the researchers observe the flasks for a while, continuing to replenish growth medium and pollutant as necessary. Predict the most likely results you would expect to see in the growth of the flasks after the allotted time",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1016691,
     "content_html": "The growth in Flask A will continuously exceed that of Flask B."
    },
    {
     "id": 1016692,
     "content_html": "The growth in Flask B will continuously exceed that of Flask A."
    },
    {
     "id": 1016693,
     "content_html": "The  differences in growth between the two flasks will eventually decrease."
    },
    {
     "id": 1016694,
     "content_html": "Eventually, there will be little to no growth in each flask."
    }
   ],
